Subject: The silent-success stubs say so, or stop being bridged

A bridge whose whole body is `g_eax = 0` (or a no-op over an xbox_* stub) returns
success without doing the job. Routed, that is invisible in a log: a title that
imports the ordinal gets a clean 0 and carries on, and the missing work surfaces
later as an unattributable failure. A stub is judged by its final implementation
(the xbox_* it calls, and any fci_* that calls), not by the bridge's shape: the
five crypto bridges (XcModExp, XcDESKeyParity, XcKeyTable, XcBlockCrypt,
XcBlockCryptCBC) are on neither list: the online branch's 0027-kernel-crypto
wires them to real DES and Montgomery exponentiation (src/fci_xcrypt.c). Q25 splits the
96 genuine stubs in kernel_bridge.c into the two cases that matter.

Imported by a title the toolkit ships for (13 of them): the call is real, so the
stub must keep answering -- unbridging it would crash the title -- but it now
prints one line the first time it is called, naming the ordinal and the work it
skips (bridge_stub_log, driven by g_stub_imported in the dispatch). Once per
process: a stub a title calls every frame must not log every frame.

Imported by no known title (83 of them): unbridge them. bridge_for_ordinal now
returns NULL for the g_stub_unimported set, so a title that ever imports one of
these takes the standard unimplemented path the toolkit already has for an
unbridged ordinal -- the fci_unbridged_ordinal hook, which in fci-recomp is
STOP 87 naming the ordinal and its caller -- instead of a success that does
nothing. Arg cleanup is unaffected: g_slot_arg_bytes comes from
stdcall_args_for_ordinal, which is independent of the bridge.

The lists are relative to the titles this toolkit is built for (the import
tables in build/lift/{default,evolution}); a new title changes them.

---
diff --git a/src/kernel/kernel_bridge.c b/src/kernel/kernel_bridge.c
--- a/src/kernel/kernel_bridge.c
+++ b/src/kernel/kernel_bridge.c
@@ -8946,8 +8946,92 @@
     }
